Take-Home AI Engineer Assessment
This assessment is designed to evaluate your approach, reasoning, and
problem-solving—not perfection.
You are welcome to attempt all sections and use any combination of tools or
APIs. It’s perfectly fine if you don’t finish everything or if some parts aren’t fully
correct.
Please focus on clarity, justification, and trade-off reasoning. Tell us why you
made your choices, what you would improve if you had more time, and how you
would extend your solution for production.
In short — do your best, explain your thinking, and have fun with it.
Section A — Architecture (Take-Home, ~60 min)
Deliverable (1–2 pages max):
Design a multitenant, production-grade “Answering over External Data” system that
combines RAG + Tools/Agents.
Address (briefly):
1. Data ingestion & indexing (chunking, embeddings, hybrid search).
2. Agentic layer: how the “planner” chooses tools (e.g., web/search, weather, currency).
3. Cost, latency & caching (prompt cache, vector cache, HTTP cache).
4. Security & multitenancy (RBAC, per-tenant data isolation, audit logging, secret handling).
5. Observability (traces, metrics, token/latency budgets, failure dashboards).
6. Deployment (containerized, CI/CD, blue/green or canary)
You may assume Azure (Functions/Container Apps + AI Search + Key Vault) or any
equivalent cloud.
Scoring (25%)
● Clear, pragmatic design
● Current best practices (2025): RAG + agent tools, evals, caching
● Realistic cost/scale/risk trade-offs

Section B — Coding (Take-Home, ~2–3 hrs)
Build a small, production-minded “Agentic QA” service that chooses and calls live APIs as
tools to answer user questions, then returns a grounded, cited answer.
Core Requirements
1. Tool-using:
○ A simple planner or agent that decides which tool(s) to call based on the user
query.
○ Supports at least two live tools;
2. Live tools (choose at least two):
○ Web search with the following:
https://api.duckduckgo.com/?q={query}&format=json&no_html=1
○ Dummy tool that returns weather data to the agent.
3. Tool schema & structured I/O:
○ Each tool exposes a clear schema (inputs, outputs).
○ The LLM (or your planner) must produce/consume JSON for tool calls and final
structured output:
{
"answer": "string",
"sources": [{"name":"string","url":"string"}],
"latency_ms": {"total": 123, "by_step": {"retrieve": 45, "llm": 60}},
"tokens": {"prompt": 0, "completion": 0}
}

4. Reasoning quality:
○ The agent explains which tools it used and cites sources in the final answer.

Tech Choices
● Python 3.10+
● You may use LangChain or Semantic Kernel—but also show your core agent loop
(planner + tool registry) in your own code.
● HTTP: httpx or requests
● Tracing/metrics: simple stdout JSON logs are fine

What to Submit
● Repo or zip with source, tests, README.md
● .env.example

Scoring (45%)
● Correct tool selection & reasoning
● Code quality & structure; tests
● Robustness (retries, timeouts, caching)
● Latency awareness & parallelism
● Clear, cited answers with structured JSON
Nice-to-Haves (Bonus Points)
● Streaming answers
● JSON Schema validation of tool I/O
● Simple policy layer (e.g., block network calls for disallowed domains)
● Dockerfile + one-liner run
● Basic concurrency limits (semaphores) for tools